  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nIssued at 200 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nWednesday night into Thursday morning, a longwave trough across the \nHudson Bay region and an upper-level ridge over the western United \nStates will maintain northwesterly flow aloft across the region. \nWednesday night currently appears mostly dry, as recent model \nguidance has backed off on the stronger forcing depicted in previous \nruns. Even the higher-end NBM guidance shows little support for \nmeasurable precipitation, with probabilities for 12-hour \nprecipitation exceeding 0.01 inch generally around 10 percent or \nless. Given the moist environment, precipitation chances will \ncontinue to be monitored, though current trends support a mainly dry \nforecast Wednesday night into Thursday morning. Overnight lows will \nremain mild, generally in the middle 50s. During the day Thursday, a \nsubtle disturbance embedded within the northwesterly flow is \nforecast to approach the area. Weak mid-level frontogenesis and \npositive vorticity advection may provide sufficient ascent for \nisolated shower and thunderstorm development. The better opportunity \nfor precipitation currently appears to favor eastern portions of the \nforecast area, though confidence in coverage remains low given \ncontinued differences among model guidance. Highs Thursday should \nreach the middle to upper 70s, with Thursday night lows ranging from \nthe middle 40s to lower 50s.\n\nFriday, an upper-level trough will move into the Pacific Northwest, \nresulting in a more progressive upper-level pattern and pushing the \nwestern ridge eastward. The ridge will amplify across the central \nUnited States as a strong lee-side surface low develops across the \nnorthern Rockies ahead of the approaching trough. Strengthening warm \nair advection will allow 850-mb temperatures to rise into the 20 to \n25\u00b0C range, while a tightening surface pressure gradient results in \nincreasing southerly low-level flow across the region. This setup \nshould result in a warm Friday, with highs currently forecast in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s. Given the magnitude of the warm air aloft \nand potential for sufficient boundary-layer mixing, current guidance \nmay be underestimating temperatures, particularly across western \nportions of the area where middle to upper 80s may be possible. \nPrecipitation chances should remain low Friday given the lack of \nupper-level forcing. Friday night lows should remain mild, generally \nin the upper 40s to lower 50s.\n\nSaturday into early next week, the upper-level trough will continue \ndigging southward from the Pacific Northwest into the southwestern \nUnited States. As the trough amplifies, southwesterly flow aloft \nwill become established across the central Plains. Model guidance \nhas begun to show better agreement in the trough eventually becoming \nseparated from the stronger northern-stream flow and evolving into a \ncutoff low over the western or southwestern United States. Such an \nevolution would favor a slower progression of the system, \npotentially delaying the arrival of the main upper-level forcing \nuntil around the middle of next week. With the main trough remaining \nwest of the region, ridging is expected to amplify once again across \nthe central United States Sunday into early next week. However, \nsubtle disturbances ejecting northeastward within the southwesterly \nflow ahead of the main system could provide occasional opportunities \nfor precipitation, particularly Sunday into early next week. \nConfidence in the timing and coverage of any precipitation remains \nlow. Saturday should remain quite warm, with highs potentially \nreaching the middle to upper 80s across much of the area. \nTemperatures should trend closer to seasonal values Sunday into \nearly next week, though the magnitude of any cool down will depend \non the evolution of the western trough. Beyond the current long-term \nperiod, the upper-level low should eventually begin progressing \neastward as the pattern becomes more progressive. This could result \nin a greater opportunity for more widespread precipitation across \nthe region. Considerable uncertainty remains regarding the timing, \ntrack, and evolution of this system, and trends will continue to be \nmonitored over the next several days.",
